1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,653
Research Computing on Explorer and AICR. Intro to Explorer and O O D. The web front

2
00:00:05,673 --> 00:00:10,972
door to the Explorer cluster: launch apps, move files and start jobs from a browser,

3
00:00:10,992 --> 00:00:12,390
with no terminal required.

4
00:00:12,460 --> 00:00:16,411
Hola. This is Liam, in for Bear. First, a correction. Explorer is not a big computer

5
00:00:16,431 --> 00:00:20,630
where you run your code. It's a cluster of computers, and you ask it for a slice.

6
00:00:20,650 --> 00:00:24,353
This lecture is the whole introduction: what a cluster is, how you get an account,

7
00:00:24,373 --> 00:00:28,076
how you log in through the terminal or the Open OnDemand website, where your files

8
00:00:28,096 --> 00:00:32,544
live, how you move data, how you run and check a job, how to right-size it, and how

9
00:00:32,564 --> 00:00:33,040
software works.

10
00:00:33,110 --> 00:00:37,211
Six terms. An HPC cluster: many computers wired together to work on one problem. A

11
00:00:37,231 --> 00:00:41,607
login node: the machine you land on, for light work only. A compute node: a machine

12
00:00:41,627 --> 00:00:45,728
that does the heavy work, handed to you on request. Slurm: the scheduler that takes

13
00:00:45,748 --> 00:00:49,849
those requests and decides who runs where. A partition: a named group of nodes with

14
00:00:49,869 --> 00:00:54,245
its own limits. And Open OnDemand, OOD: a website that gives you the cluster in a

15
00:00:54,265 --> 00:00:54,520
browser.

16
00:00:54,590 --> 00:00:59,070
Start with the shape of the thing. A cluster is many computers working together on

17
00:00:59,090 --> 00:01:04,170
problems too big for one desk. You log in to a login node. Light work happens there:

18
00:01:04,190 --> 00:01:08,370
editing a file, looking around. When you need real computing power, the work moves

19
00:01:08,390 --> 00:01:12,270
to a compute node, and a scheduler called Slurm manages everyone's requests for

20
00:01:12,290 --> 00:01:12,870
those nodes.

21
00:01:12,940 --> 00:01:17,485
Explorer is Northeastern's cluster, run by the Research Computing team, and it

22
00:01:17,505 --> 00:01:22,050
replaced the older Discovery cluster. Forty-five thousand CPU cores. More than five

23
00:01:22,070 --> 00:01:26,615
hundred twenty-five GPUs. Free of charge to every Northeastern faculty member and

24
00:01:26,635 --> 00:01:31,180
student. It lives at the Massachusetts Green High Performance Computing Center in

25
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:31,560
Holyoke.

26
00:01:31,630 --> 00:01:36,288
Around the compute: a ten-gigabit link to the university network for moving data in

27
00:01:36,308 --> 00:01:40,633
and out, six petabytes of fast shared storage, and between the nodes themselves

28
00:01:40,652 --> 00:01:44,977
either ten-gigabit ethernet or InfiniBand at one or two hundred gigabits a second,

29
00:01:44,997 --> 00:01:47,650
so every size of workload has a place.

30
00:01:47,720 --> 00:01:52,207
Getting on. For research, every user belongs to a storage space owned by a PI or

31
00:01:52,227 --> 00:01:56,432
staff member; storage is free up to thirty-five terabytes. If your lab has one, the

32
00:01:56,452 --> 00:02:00,939
PI adds you with the project command, or you file an access request. If it doesn't,

33
00:02:00,959 --> 00:02:04,320
the PI requests a storage space first. For coursework, your instructor applies

34
00:02:04,340 --> 00:02:07,700
through the classroom access form, and students get in through the course.

35
00:02:07,770 --> 00:02:12,298
Two ways in. The terminal, and the Open OnDemand website. Both reach the same

36
00:02:12,318 --> 00:02:16,845
cluster. The team's advice is to get comfortable with the terminal, and we'll use

37
00:02:16,865 --> 00:02:17,170
both.

38
00:02:17,240 --> 00:02:22,955
From a Mac, open Terminal and s s h to login dot explorer dot northeastern dot e d u

39
00:02:22,975 --> 00:02:26,879
with your Northeastern username. It asks for your password; the cursor doesn't move

40
00:02:26,899 --> 00:02:31,407
while you type it, which is normal. The welcome banner means you're on. On Windows,

41
00:02:31,427 --> 00:02:36,538
install an SSH client such as MobaXterm or PuTTY and point it at the same host, port

42
00:02:36,558 --> 00:02:36,840
twenty-two.

43
00:02:36,910 --> 00:02:43,311
Or open a browser at o o d dot explorer dot northeastern dot e d u. You sign in with

44
00:02:43,331 --> 00:02:47,484
your Northeastern username and password, confirm with Duo, and land on the Open

45
00:02:47,504 --> 00:02:49,410
OnDemand dashboard. You're on the cluster.

46
00:02:49,480 --> 00:02:53,309
Now, where things live. Everyone has a home directory and a scratch directory.

47
00:02:53,329 --> 00:02:57,454
Researchers also have a projects space. Students in a course have a courses space,

48
00:02:57,474 --> 00:03:02,191
and may not have projects. Always write the full path, starting with a slash, so a

49
00:03:02,211 --> 00:03:04,560
script means the same place wherever it runs.

50
00:03:04,630 --> 00:03:08,567
Each has a size. Home holds seventy-five gigabytes, and it fills fastest; when it

51
00:03:08,587 --> 00:03:12,242
does, you'll see quota exceeded. Scratch holds fifty terabytes, but it's purged on

52
00:03:12,262 --> 00:03:16,765
the first Tuesday of every month, so it's no place for anything you want to keep.

53
00:03:16,785 --> 00:03:21,288
Projects is the long-term home for big data and for folders of many small files. A

54
00:03:21,308 --> 00:03:24,680
course space holds one to five terabytes, shared by the whole class.

55
00:03:24,750 --> 00:03:29,551
Paths in practice. L S lists a directory, and cat prints a file. With an absolute

56
00:03:29,571 --> 00:03:34,070
path you can read any file you have permission for, from anywhere on the cluster.

57
00:03:34,140 --> 00:03:38,791
Moving data in. From your own machine, S C P copies a file to the cluster through

58
00:03:38,811 --> 00:03:42,912
the transfer node. Make a small test file, copy it into your home directory, then

59
00:03:42,932 --> 00:03:48,132
log in and l s to see it arrive. To put it in your project space instead, change the

60
00:03:48,152 --> 00:03:50,880
destination path. Many small files? Zip or tar them first.

61
00:03:50,950 --> 00:03:55,805
Or use the website. On the Open OnDemand dashboard, open Files, then Home Directory.

62
00:03:55,825 --> 00:04:00,679
The Files app starts in your home directory, and the Change directory button takes

63
00:04:00,699 --> 00:04:02,420
you to scratch or projects.

64
00:04:02,490 --> 00:04:07,823
Upload opens a window where you browse for files or drop them in. The Files app is

65
00:04:07,843 --> 00:04:12,861
limited to ten gigabytes a transfer. Bigger than that, use S C P; for very large

66
00:04:12,881 --> 00:04:16,955
datasets, Globus. And the same app downloads files, edits them, and makes new

67
00:04:16,975 --> 00:04:17,270
folders.

68
00:04:17,340 --> 00:04:22,263
Running jobs. So far we've only used the login nodes and the transfer node. Real

69
00:04:22,283 --> 00:04:26,877
computation has to move to a compute node: no scientific software, big transfers or

70
00:04:26,897 --> 00:04:31,490
heavy work on the login node, because its traffic slows the whole cluster for

71
00:04:31,510 --> 00:04:31,820
everyone.

72
00:04:31,890 --> 00:04:36,745
From the terminal, s run gets you a compute node. The simplest form takes the

73
00:04:36,765 --> 00:04:41,620
defaults: four hours, one node, one task, two gigabytes of memory, two CPUs, on the

74
00:04:41,640 --> 00:04:45,520
short partition. Flags ask for more: here, six hours, fifty gigabytes, sixteen

75
00:04:45,540 --> 00:04:48,120
tasks. Every flag has a short form too.

76
00:04:48,190 --> 00:04:53,760
A partition is how the team groups nodes: by time limit, by node count, by CPU or

77
00:04:53,780 --> 00:04:57,706
GPU. On Explorer: short, the default, and sharing for CPU work. gpu,

78
00:04:57,726 --> 00:05:01,981
gpu-interactive, gpu-short and sharing for GPU work. Two you apply for: long and

79
00:05:02,001 --> 00:05:06,914
multigpu, once you can show your code uses them well. And two only for courses:

80
00:05:06,934 --> 00:05:07,900
courses and courses-gpu.

81
00:05:07,970 --> 00:05:14,090
Which one? If you're in a course and have a courses directory, use courses for CPU

82
00:05:14,110 --> 00:05:18,694
jobs and courses-gpu for GPU jobs. Otherwise, short for most CPU work;

83
00:05:18,714 --> 00:05:23,299
gpu-interactive or gpu-short for interactive GPU sessions; and the gpu partition for

84
00:05:23,319 --> 00:05:24,450
GPU batch jobs.

85
00:05:24,520 --> 00:05:28,820
A GPU is a processor built for thousands of parallel tasks, and it's what AI and

86
00:05:28,840 --> 00:05:32,870
much of modern science run on. To get one interactively, name the partition and the

87
00:05:32,890 --> 00:05:37,460
g r e s. Naming the card type gives you exactly that card. Leaving it general gives

88
00:05:37,480 --> 00:05:41,780
you whatever is free first, fine for a quick test, but for repeated runs use the

89
00:05:41,800 --> 00:05:44,480
same type, or at least record which one you got.

90
00:05:44,550 --> 00:05:49,563
On the website, an interactive job is a form. For RStudio you pick the version, the

91
00:05:49,583 --> 00:05:53,966
partition, the hours, the CPUs and the memory, and press Launch. The defaults are

92
00:05:53,986 --> 00:05:54,910
fine to start.

93
00:05:54,980 --> 00:05:59,262
The session moves from Queued to Starting to Running, and your time starts when it's

94
00:05:59,282 --> 00:06:03,565
running. The card shows the host node, a Cancel button, the time remaining and a

95
00:06:03,585 --> 00:06:07,580
session ID. Cancel it when you're done; that frees the node for someone else.

96
00:06:07,650 --> 00:06:12,431
The jobs that do most of the real work are batch jobs: set up, submitted, and run

97
00:06:12,451 --> 00:06:16,950
without you. Test your commands in an s run session first; then write a script. The

98
00:06:16,970 --> 00:06:21,469
S BATCH lines name the job, the partition, one node and sixteen tasks, and ask for

99
00:06:21,489 --> 00:06:25,140
email on every event. Then load your software, activate your environment, and run.

100
00:06:25,210 --> 00:06:29,811
Submit it with s batch. Slurm answers with a job ID. When the job runs, its output

101
00:06:29,831 --> 00:06:34,432
goes to a file named slurm, dash, the job ID, dot out; cat shows what the job

102
00:06:34,452 --> 00:06:38,782
printed. Use less to page through long files, and q to quit. If you're unsure how

103
00:06:38,802 --> 00:06:44,218
much to ask for, ask for a little and scale up when a job runs out of memory, CPU or

104
00:06:44,238 --> 00:06:44,490
time.

105
00:06:44,560 --> 00:06:48,697
Is it running? s queue with your username lists your jobs: ID, partition, name,

106
00:06:48,717 --> 00:06:53,745
state, R for running, time and node. s queue for a partition, piped to w c, counts

107
00:06:53,765 --> 00:06:57,902
how busy the queue is: five hundred eighteen lines here. And dash dash start

108
00:06:57,922 --> 00:06:59,980
estimates when a waiting job will begin.

109
00:07:00,050 --> 00:07:04,888
On the website, Jobs, then Active Jobs, shows the same list. And My Interactive

110
00:07:04,908 --> 00:07:09,399
Sessions shows each session with its host node and, in parentheses, the seven-digit

111
00:07:09,419 --> 00:07:13,910
Slurm job ID you'd give the Research Computing team if something goes wrong.

112
00:07:13,980 --> 00:07:18,396
Click the Session ID link and you'll see the files Open OnDemand made for the job.

113
00:07:18,416 --> 00:07:23,109
output dot log is the one to read when a session doesn't behave, and the one the

114
00:07:23,129 --> 00:07:25,050
team will ask for in a ticket.

115
00:07:25,120 --> 00:07:30,110
Now the habit that makes you a power user. Ask for close to the minimum you need,

116
00:07:30,130 --> 00:07:34,531
because the more you request, the longer you wait for it. After a job finishes,

117
00:07:34,551 --> 00:07:38,362
seff, Slurm efficiency, shows what it actually used. Two real reports from the

118
00:07:38,382 --> 00:07:42,193
training. Job one: twelve cores, eight percent CPU efficiency, ten percent of fifty

119
00:07:42,213 --> 00:07:46,319
gigabytes used. It could have asked for far less. Job two: eight cores at

120
00:07:46,339 --> 00:07:50,151
eighty-four percent CPU efficiency. A much better fit. Try seff on your next

121
00:07:50,171 --> 00:07:50,740
finished job.

122
00:07:50,810 --> 00:07:55,584
Software comes as modules, so you load only what you need, when you need it, because

123
00:07:55,604 --> 00:07:59,779
some conflict. module avail lists them; module load loads one, with the exact name

124
00:07:59,799 --> 00:08:04,573
and case you saw. Don't put module load in your dot bashrc: it interferes with Open

125
00:08:04,593 --> 00:08:06,970
OnDemand and makes your work harder to reproduce.

126
00:08:07,040 --> 00:08:10,966
Beyond modules, three routes. Conda environments, which can hold many kinds of

127
00:08:10,986 --> 00:08:14,582
scientific software, not only Python. Containers, which the cluster supports. And

128
00:08:14,602 --> 00:08:19,843
building from source. And when you're stuck: email r c help at northeastern dot e d

129
00:08:19,863 --> 00:08:23,460
u, come to office hours on Zoom, or book a consultation.

130
00:08:23,530 --> 00:08:28,676
Let's recap with Claude. A cluster: you log in to a login node and ask Slurm for a

131
00:08:28,696 --> 00:08:33,269
compute node. Two doors: s s h to the login address, or the Open OnDemand website.

132
00:08:33,289 --> 00:08:36,426
Home holds seventy-five gigabytes; scratch holds fifty terabytes and is purged

133
00:08:36,446 --> 00:08:41,592
monthly; projects is for the long term. s run for a live node, s batch for a script,

134
00:08:41,612 --> 00:08:45,898
S queue to check on it. Partitions: short by default, the gpu partitions for GPUs,

135
00:08:45,918 --> 00:08:50,203
courses for classes. And seff after every job, so you request close to what you

136
00:08:50,223 --> 00:08:50,490
need.

137
00:08:50,560 --> 00:08:55,020
Your turn. Paste this into Claude: I'm new to a Slurm cluster. Here's what I want to

138
00:08:55,040 --> 00:08:58,974
run: [describe your program and data in two sentences]. Tell me which of my files

139
00:08:58,994 --> 00:09:02,663
belong in home, scratch and projects; write me an srun command to test it

140
00:09:02,683 --> 00:09:06,090
interactively with modest resources; then write the sbatch script for the real run,

141
00:09:06,110 --> 00:09:10,570
and tell me which commands I use to check on the job and to read its efficiency

142
00:09:10,590 --> 00:09:14,260
afterwards. Then check two things yourself. Does every path it wrote start with a

143
00:09:14,280 --> 00:09:18,740
slash? And did it tell you to test with s run before you submit with s batch?

144
00:09:18,810 --> 00:09:23,540
Intro to Explorer and OOD. At Nik Bear Brown.
